Title: "Subsets"
Id: "d1d71d30-1ce6-427e-bbc4-dd3ccf53fe53"


Text: "Beyond membership, the fundamental comparison between two sets is
       containment. It is vital to distinguish between primitive membership
       ($x \in A$) and inclusion ($A \subseteq B$): membership relates an object
       to a collection and is non-transitive, whereas inclusion is a universal
       relation between sets that is both reflexive and transitive:"
Id: "7bfbdad3-bf51-4e2d-ae06-8dd2fa3a220d"


[A \:subset:/ B]
Declares: A is \set
when: B is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: X "in" A
  then: X "in"? B
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" A :-> x_ "in" B
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ is a subset of $B?$"
. written: "A? \subseteq B?"
. description: "Universal containment relation $A \subseteq B$: every element
                $x \in A$ satisfies $x \in B$. Imposes a canonical Boolean
                lattice structure on power sets and acts as the categorical
                subobject arrow in Set."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
Id: "2b2b89a1-d106-4377-ae48-14c704063e5e"


Text: "When containment holds without equality, the smaller set is strictly
       contained within the larger, guaranteeing the existence of elements in
       the ambient set that do not belong to the subset:"
Id: "c27fbf42-311e-4efd-b4c8-2da3ad6370a7"


[A \:(proper)::subset:/ B]
Refines: A
when: B is \set
specifies: A \:subset:/ B
satisfies: A != B
Documented:
. adjective: "proper"
. written: "A? \subsetneq B?"
. description: "Strict containment $A \subsetneq B$: combines subset inclusion
                with non-equality, guaranteeing the existence of an ambient
                witness $b \in B \setminus A$."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
Id: "7d070f37-61a5-4c62-b8da-4831c222e4b2"


Text: "The universal nature of subset inclusion ensures that containment can be
       chained: whenever one collection is nested inside a second, and that
       second within a third, the first is necessarily contained within the
       third:"
Id: "370b0f76-ab0f-4f7f-80fa-120bb7ffe9e2"


Theorem:
given:
. C is \set
. B \:subset:/ C
. A \:subset:/ B
then:
. A \:subset?:/ C
Documented:
. called: "Transitivity of Subset Relation"
. description: "Syllogistic chaining of containment: transitivity ensures that
                subset inclusion forms a preorder on any collection of sets, and
                a partial order modulo extensionality."
Proof: """Assume $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq C$. Let $x \in A$ be an
          arbitrary element. By definition of $A \subseteq B$, the condition
          $x \in A$ implies $x \in B$. Since $x \in B$, the condition
          $B \subseteq C$ similarly implies $x \in C$. Because $x \in A$ was
          arbitrary, every element of $A$ belongs to $C$, which establishes
          $A \subseteq C$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
Id: "6a4bcb50-97bc-4d1b-a9b2-750c963bb720"


Text: "Connecting subset inclusion back to Extensionality yields the
       double-containment principle, the standard technique for establishing the
       identity of two sets by demonstrating that each is contained within the
       other:"
Id: "a0b6d62f-1f1d-4be5-997b-40aa03cc4ac7"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then: A = B
iff:
. A \:subset?:/ B
. B \:subset?:/ A
Documented:
. called: "Set Equality via Mutual Inclusion"
. description: "The fundamental double-containment proof technique: establishes
                $A = B$ by independently proving $A \subseteq B$ and
                $B \subseteq A$, decomposing extensional identity into two
                universal implications."
Proof: """By the Axiom of Extensionality, $A = B$ if and only if for all $x$,
          $x \in A \iff x \in B$. Decomposing this universal biconditional into
          its two conditional directions yields
          $(\forall x, x \in A \implies x \in B) \land (\forall x, x \in B \implies x \in A)$.
          The first conjunct is precisely the definition of $A \subseteq B$, and
          the second conjunct is the definition of $B \subseteq A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
Id: "d3ee9362-d0be-47f0-bf0e-ecea7416d100"


SectionTitle: "The Inclusion Order"
Id: "2c6cb7fb-49cd-41a9-8ce3-c143e7cc045c"


Text: "Restricting subset inclusion to the power set $\mathcal{P}(U)$ of an
       ambient universe endows the collection of subsets with the structure of a
       bounded partially ordered set, bounded below by the empty set and above
       by the universe itself:"
Id: "d74deced-879a-4cb2-8af3-d10013469765"


[\inclusion.order:on{U}]
Defines: R is \partial.order:on{\power.set:of{U}}
when: U is \set
expresses:
. forAll: A, B "in" \power.set:of{U}
  then:
  . have: R(A, B)
    iff: A \:subset?:/ B
. exists: E "in" \power.set:of{U}
  suchThat:
  . E = \empty.set
  . forAll: A "in" \power.set:of{U}
    then: R(E, A)
. exists: T "in" \power.set:of{U}
  suchThat:
  . T = U
  . forAll: A "in" \power.set:of{U}
    then: R(A, T)
Documented:
. called: "inclusion order on the power set of $U?$"
. description: "The canonical Boolean algebra partial order
                $(\mathcal{P}(U), \subseteq)$. Features $\emptyset$ as the
                universal bottom element ($\bot$) and $U$ as the universal top
                element ($\top$), with set operations realizing meet, join, and
                complementation."
Id: "ff6d7e2f-1927-4d30-b771-34ea8726711c"


Text: "In advanced mathematics, one rarely studies the entire power set in
       isolation. Instead, structures such as topologies, $\sigma$-algebras, and
       filters isolate subcollections satisfying specific closure properties. We
       declare such collections as families of subsets:"
Id: "097603ca-df29-48a5-82e2-666309f59b91"


[\family.of.subsets:of{U}]
Declares: A \:subset:/ \power.set:of{U}
when: U is \set
Documented:
. called: "family of subsets of $U?$"
. description: "A higher-order collection $\mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(U)$
                of subsets of $U$. Provides the structural substrate for
                topologies, $\sigma$-algebras, uniformities, and filter bases."
Id: "df5630a9-0ac4-4920-995a-7002bdccbf15"
